TERM - Computah OS library
(generated from docs/API.md)

The terminal: text with a cursor, wrapping and
scrolling on the primary GPU and screen, and line
input from the keyboards attached to that screen.

term.write(value[, wrap])

Writes text at the cursor and moves it. wrap
(default true) wraps at the right edge.
Understands \n, \r, \t, \b and ANSI colour codes
ESC[...m (0 reset, 1 bold, 3 italic, 4 underline,
5 blink, 7 inverse, 9 strikethrough, 30–37/90–97
foreground, 40–47/100–107 background, 39/49
default colours).

  term.write("\27[31mError:\27[0m something failed\n")

term.read([options]) -> string

Reads a line with editing: arrows, Home/End,
Backspace/Delete, Up/Down for history, Tab for
completion, Ctrl+Shift+V paste. Returns the line
without the newline, "" after Ctrl+C, or nil after
Ctrl+D on an empty line (and when there is no
screen). Options table:

- history (table): previous lines; the new line is
  appended.
- complete (function(line, cursorPos) -> list):
  completions cycled with Tab.
- pwchar (string): shown instead of each typed
  character.
- nobreak (boolean): don't move to a new line at
  the end.

term.pull([timeout,] [name,] ...) -> name, ...

event.pull with a blinking cursor shown at the
cursor position while waiting.

term.clear()

Clears the screen and puts the cursor at the top
left.

term.clearLine()

Clears the cursor's line and moves the cursor to
its start.

term.getCursor() -> x, y

The cursor position.

term.setCursor(x, y)

Moves the cursor.

term.getCursorBlink() -> boolean

Whether the cursor blinks while reading input.

term.setCursorBlink(enabled)

Turns cursor blinking on or off.

term.getViewport() -> width, height

The terminal's size in characters.

term.getGlobalArea() -> x, y, width, height

The terminal's area on the screen (always the
whole screen: 1, 1, width, height).

term.scroll([lines])

Scrolls the text up (default one line).

term.isAvailable() -> boolean

Whether the terminal has a GPU and screen.

term.gpu() -> proxy

The terminal's GPU.

term.screen() -> address

The terminal's screen.

term.keyboard() -> address

The first keyboard attached to the terminal's
screen, or nil.

term.keyboards() -> table

All keyboards attached to the terminal's screen.

term.bind([gpu[, screen]]) -> boolean

Binds the terminal to a GPU and screen (addresses;
default the primary ones), sets the maximum
resolution and clears the screen. Happens
automatically at boot and when a GPU or screen
appears.
